course: dictionary
lesson: 12178
title: Proof beyond doubt
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When something is proved conclusively, there is no doubt left. «The test proved conclusively that the plant needs light to grow.» After the test, nobody can argue about it.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. Proved means shown to be true, so conclusively tells you how well it was shown: completely, with nothing left to question.
explain: Scientists, maths teachers and detectives use this word a lot. «The fingerprints showed conclusively that Sam had been in the room.» The fingerprints leave no room for doubt.
explain: To check your meaning, swap in «for certain» or «beyond doubt». If the sentence still makes sense, you have the right idea.
fact: Conclusively is an adverb, so it tells you how something is proved or done.
fact: «Conclusively» means in a way that proves something completely, with no doubt left.
fact: It can also mean in a final way that settles the matter.
fact: Useful phrases: prove conclusively, show conclusively, conclusively won.
fact: «Beyond doubt» and «for certain» are close in meaning, but conclusively is more formal and is often used in science and detective stories.
exercise: «The test proved conclusively that the plant needs light.» What does conclusively mean here? | Quickly and loudly | Unhappily | *Completely, with no doubt left | Only just
exercise: «The fingerprints showed conclusively that Sam had been in the room.» What does conclusively tell you? | Sam was never in the room | *Sam was there, and the proof is certain | Sam might have been there, but nobody knows | Sam was tired after the walk
exercise: «Our team won conclusively, scoring five goals to none.» Find and copy the adverb that tells how the team won. | goals | *conclusively | five | team
test: «The scientist's results showed conclusively that the medicine worked.» What does conclusively mean? | *With no doubt left, completely proved | For no good reason | With a loud noise | Slowly and carefully
test: Which sentence uses conclusively correctly? | *The video proved conclusively that the ball was out. | The cat conclusively sat on the mat, but maybe not. | She conclusively likes to walk to school. | Tom conclusively ate his lunch quickly.
exam: Hana's notes showed conclusively that the bridge was safe. Which phrase means the same as conclusively? | Once in a while | *Beyond doubt | Very slowly | Out of breath
